I just want to say, hear hear.

Nethack is the only computer game I know of that implements shops in
this strange, but incredibly cool, way.  Not even other Roguelikes are
clever enough to make shops actual *rooms* in the dungeon, with a
shopkeeper monster presiding over them.  That's one of the primary
features of Nethack, to me, that it's insightful enough to do this, as
it adds incredible richness to the game, while other games, whether
cooked up by some guy in his basement or created by hundreds of
developers working on behalf of a billion-dollar corporation, just
give the player a damn menu.

The reason other games don't do it is that yes, it is open to abuse
unless the developers have some Big Developmental Power, and most
don't, and know it.  Also, they fail to see what complexity this would
bring to the game.  The DevTeam, obviously, has strong mojo, and has
made the right decision.

But treating shops like this, logically, presents the possibility of
stealing, or other forms of abuse.  Good.  Abusing shops isn't a bug,
it's a feature, and some of the penalties for clever players trying
things (I'm thinking specifically of shopkeepers grabbing your
backpack if you jump down a hole, regardless of your proximity to
them) obviously make them too powerful.

It probably shouldn't be that easy to steal from shops, but I question
the current way it's handled.

